{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793692726,"nonce":"be10RrSSt0ok7S5kqCiMiymS1+U=","message":"Local test of one candidate, on the NUC (16 threads) with the process restricted by taskset so available_parallelism() is what a runner reports: the optimizing compile's wait for the baseline tier (wait_for_baseline_quiet, up to 250 ms, counts baseline compiles in flight against the cores) is not the cause. Removing the wait (ZYNTAX_OPTIMIZE_QUIET_MS=0) did not help and made it worse when CPUs were scarce: crypto_pyaes steady state 8.6 ms with the wait against 12.6 ms without at 2 CPUs, 18.7 against 25.6 at 1 CPU. What dominates is how many CPUs the process effectively has: telco steady state is 3.8 ms at 4 CPUs, 6.7 at 2 (3.3 to 7.9 across runs) and 9.4 at 1; crypto_pyaes 8.0, 8.6, 18.7. The EPYC CI figures (telco 9.7 ms against 4.9 on Intel; crypto 11 or 27 ms on the same model, flat inside each run) look like a runner delivering the compute of one to two CPUs, not four, at the time of the run. Every Linux runner seen reports 4 vCPUs and 15 GiB regardless of vendor, so memory does not separate them. Next: the diagnostic workflow, which measures effective parallel throughput, SMT sibling cost and steal time on the runners themselves.","files":null}]}